WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.260
ISO/IEC 27018 sits within a coordinated standards ecosystem.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:19.080
ISO/IEC 27001 defines requirements for establishing, implementing, maintaining, and continually improving an Information Security Management System, or ISMS.

3
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.220
It is the management-system requirements standard.

4
00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:28.560
ISO/IEC 27002 provides information security control guidance.

5
00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:39.140
ISO/IEC 27018 uses that control-guidance foundation and adapts it to the narrower problem of PII in public cloud services where the provider acts as a PII processor.

6
00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:45.180
ISO/IEC 27017 is related but different.

7
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:51.200
It addresses cloud-service information security controls for both cloud service providers and cloud service customers.

8
00:00:51.300 --> 00:01:01.460
Its scope is broader cloud security: responsibilities, virtualization, cloud administration, monitoring, and service use.

9
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:07.880
ISO/IEC 27018 is narrower and more privacy-specific.

10
00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:11.820
It focuses on PII protection in public cloud processing relationships.

11
00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:19.120
A provider can need both: cloud security guidance for the service and processor-focused privacy guidance for customer PII.

12
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:25.640
ISO/IEC 27701 addresses a Privacy Information Management System, or PIMS.

13
00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:41.100
The current ISO catalogue identifies ISO/IEC 27701:2025 as a standard for establishing, implementing, maintaining, and improving a PIMS, with requirements and guidance for PII controllers and processors.

14
00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:49.680
That broader privacy governance can complement ISO/IEC 27018, but the standards are not interchangeable.

15
00:01:49.780 --> 00:02:01.220
ISO/IEC 27701 addresses privacy management across an organization; ISO/IEC 27018 focuses on public cloud PII processor guidance.

16
00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:07.180
ISO/IEC 29100 provides a privacy framework.

17
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:21.920
The current ISO/IEC 29100:2024 edition establishes common privacy terminology, describes actors and roles in PII processing, addresses safeguarding considerations, and references privacy principles for information technology.

18
00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:24.820
That makes it useful for a shared vocabulary.

19
00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:38.680
ISO/IEC 27005 can support information security risk management, and ISO/IEC 27555 offers guidance on policies and procedures for PII deletion.

20
00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:47.340
These related standards help, but none removes the need to define the actual cloud service, role, data, risk, and evidence.

21
00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.820
Requirements and guidance should not be blurred.

22
00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:58.520
A management-system requirement defines what an organization needs to establish and operate if it is claiming conformity to that management system.

23
00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:02.220
Control guidance helps select and implement measures.

24
00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.760
Legal obligations arise from law and regulation.

25
00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.820
Contractual obligations arise from agreements.

26
00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:14.920
Customer requirements and organization-specific risk treatments may add further expectations.

27
00:03:15.020 --> 00:03:20.300
A defensible program keeps these sources distinct while tracing how they influence one another.

28
00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:33.380
A crosswalk can be valuable when it maps business service to PII processing activity, role, obligation, risk, control, implementation, evidence, and review result.

29
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:38.440
The limitation is that a crosswalk cannot make different standards mean the same thing.

30
00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:46.860
It cannot turn a guideline into a management-system requirement, cannot create a legal conclusion, and cannot substitute for scope analysis.

31
00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:59.840
The practical goal is traceability: stakeholders should be able to see why a control exists, what risk or obligation it supports, how it operates, and what evidence demonstrates that operation.

32
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.240
This ecosystem view also prevents overclaiming.

33
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:26.380
A provider may operate an ISMS aligned with ISO/IEC 27001, use ISO/IEC 27002 control guidance, apply ISO/IEC 27017 cloud security practices, operate privacy governance influenced by ISO/IEC 27701, and use ISO/IEC 27018 for public cloud PII processor concerns.

34
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.200
Those activities can reinforce one another, but each has its own scope and evidence.

35
00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:42.420
A mature program states the relationship plainly instead of using one standard name as shorthand for every security, privacy, legal, and contractual expectation.

36
00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:49.660
Practically, standards relationships should be managed in the same repository or governance system as risks and evidence.

37
00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:58.160
When an obligation or control changes, the organization can see affected services, owners, procedures, contracts, and assurance records.

38
00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:01.380
That makes the ecosystem useful rather than decorative.

39
00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:09.400
It also helps teams avoid audit theater, because every mapped item has to connect to a real processing activity, a real decision, and a real record.

40
00:05:09.500 --> 00:05:15.740
The map becomes a working control surface for privacy governance rather than a spreadsheet used only during assessment season.
